The '''Baliscan miracle''' ({{langx|bl|Milagro baliscano}}) is a term used to refer to the [[Balisca|confederation's]] inward-looking development strategy that produced sustained [[W:Economic growth|economic growth]] following the [[Great Adonian War]]. It is considered to be a [[W:Golden age (metaphor)|golden age]] in Balisca's history in which the [[Economy of Balisca|Baliscan economy]] grew 6.8% each year. This economic boom roughly began in 1947, and would last for the next three decades, only coming to an end with the outbreak of the [[Camboriú War]] and the onset of the [[Special Period]]. This era saw a dramatic rise in the [[W:Standard of living|standard of living]] for Baliscans and extensive infrastructure development, laying the foundation for the nation's later position as a global technological and social leader.

== Conditions for sustained growth ==
Like many nations that participated in the war effort, Balisca benefited from a general global economic recovery and the redirection of resources from [[W:War economy|wartime production]] to civilian infrastructure. The [[Confederal government of Balisca|confederal government]] initiated ambitious public works projects, and this included extensive [[W:Urban renewal|urban redevelopment]] and [[W:Public housing|public housing projects]] built throughout the 1950s and 1960s. A notable initiative, the Ten-Year Housing Programme (1953), aimed to construct enough "satisfactory housing" for an additional 5 million people within a decade.

Additionally, monumental investments were made in transportation and energy. The country developed the [[AVB|first high-speed rail network]] in the Western Hemisphere by 1969, which was rapidly expanded. Urban {{W|rapid transit}} and [[w:Commuter rail|commuter rail]] systems also saw widespread expansion, alongside the construction of [[w:Dam|dams]] (for both artificial [[W:Reservoir|reservoirs]] and [[W:Hydroelectricity|hydroelectric generation]]) and [[w:Nuclear power station|nuclear power plants]] to meet rising energy demands.

Balisca's "[[W:Free migration|free migration]]" policy regarding [[Immigration to Balisca|immigration]] and its non-aligned status made it an attractive destination for new populations, and allowed it to forge beneficial trade and diplomatic ties that were not constrained by [[w:Cold War|Cold War]] bloc alignments, potentially diversifying its economic partners and sources of technology. From the 1960s onwards, the nation experienced a significant influx of migrants, including hundreds of thousands of [[Ethnic groups in Kaftia|Kaftians]] and [[w:Political refugee|political refugees]] from other Abyalan countries. This provided a diverse labor supply to support the expanding economy.

